\description{
These functions copy or move items in a SharePoint document library. Their
interface is based on \code{\link[fs:file_copy]{fs::file_copy()}}, \code{\link[fs:file_move]{fs::file_move()}}, and
\code{\link[fs:dir_copy]{fs::dir_copy()}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=sp_item_copy]{sp_item_copy()}} copies files. Use \code{\link[=sp_dir_copy]{sp_dir_copy()}} to copy folders.
\item \code{\link[=sp_item_move]{sp_item_move()}} moves (or renames) files or folders.
\item \code{\link[=sp_dir_copy]{sp_dir_copy()}} and \code{\link[=sp_dir_move]{sp_dir_move()}} copy or move folders and their
contents.
}

As with \code{{fs}}, if \code{new_path} is an existing folder, each item is placed
inside that folder and keeps its name. Otherwise, \code{new_path} is the full
path for the new item and its parent folder must already exist. A \code{new_path}
that ends in \code{"/"} must be an existing folder.

\code{new_path} can be a path relative to the drive that holds the item, or a
SharePoint folder or document library URL. Items can be copied to another
drive or site, but they can only be moved within the same drive.
}
\details{
Copying is asynchronous

Microsoft Graph copies items in the background. If \code{wait = TRUE} (the
default), \code{\link[=sp_item_copy]{sp_item_copy()}} and \code{\link[=sp_dir_copy]{sp_dir_copy()}} check the status of each copy
until it is complete, so they can report an error if a copy fails. Large
folders can take a while to copy. If \code{wait = FALSE}, the copy may still be
running when the function returns, and a failed copy isn't reported.
}
